-- Rewrites the drone and robotic-arm projects from their repositories (and the CV), with real screenshots/renders,
-- removes the superseded 'Advanced Robotics Research' and '3-Axis Robotic Arm' projects, reorders projects,
-- and moves legacy images that pointed at the dead manus.space host into the portfolio-media bucket.

update public.projects set
  title = $p$Autonomous Cave Exploration & Obstacle-Avoidance Drone$p$,
  subtitle = $p$A ROS 2 drone that maps a GPS-denied cave in 3D, plans a safe route and decides where to explore next, all on its own.$p$,
  summary = $p$A simulated drone that explores caves, tunnels and mines without GPS. It builds a live 3D map from a LiDAR, plans collision-free routes through it, picks its own next target and recovers when it gets stuck.$p$,
  description = $p$Caves, tunnels and mines have no GPS, little light and tight walls. This project is the software brain for a drone that can enter such a place by itself: it maps what it sees in 3D, plans a safe path, chooses where to explore next and steers along the path without touching the walls.

It is built on ROS 2 Humble and Ignition Gazebo Fortress with a quadrotor carrying a 3D LiDAR, and has been developed and tested in simulation.$p$,
  status = $p$Simulation project$p$,
  start_date = $p$Nov 2025$p$,
  end_date = $p$Dec 2025$p$,
  tags = array[$p$ROS 2$p$,$p$Autonomous UAV$p$,$p$3D Mapping$p$,$p$Path Planning$p$,$p$Frontier Exploration$p$,$p$GPS-Denied Navigation$p$,$p$Obstacle Avoidance$p$]::text[],
  tools = array[$p$ROS 2 Humble$p$,$p$Ignition Gazebo Fortress$p$,$p$ros_gz bridge$p$,$p$3D LiDAR$p$,$p$OctoMap$p$,$p$D* Lite (DSP planner)$p$,$p$TF2$p$,$p$RViz2$p$,$p$C++$p$,$p$Python$p$,$p$Ubuntu 22.04$p$]::text[],
  outcomes = array[$p$Builds a live 3D map of GPS-denied tunnels from LiDAR data$p$,$p$Plans collision-free routes in 3D and repairs them as the map changes$p$,$p$Chooses its own exploration targets with no human input$p$,$p$Detects when it is stuck or about to collide and backs out of the situation$p$]::text[],
  repository_url = $p$https://github.com/MohammedBajhaw/drone-navigation$p$,
  featured = true,
  rich_content = $p$<h2>What the project is about</h2><p>A drone flying through a cave cannot use GPS, cannot count on light, and has walls only a metre or two away on every side. This project gives it a way to cope: it builds a 3D map while it flies, plans a safe path through that map, chooses where to explore next and steers along the path on its own. Everything was developed and tested in simulation.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/tunnel-octomap.jpg" alt="Coloured 3D voxel map of a tunnel built live from the drone's LiDAR" /><figcaption>The tunnel as the drone sees it: a 3D map built live from LiDAR, coloured by height.</figcaption></figure><h2>How it works</h2><ul><li><strong>Mapping:</strong> a 3D LiDAR on the drone feeds OctoMap, which turns the laser points into a grid of small cubes marking free and occupied space.</li><li><strong>Planning:</strong> a D* Lite planner searches that 3D grid for a route with no collisions, and repairs the route when new obstacles appear instead of starting again.</li><li><strong>Exploring:</strong> a frontier explorer finds the edge between mapped and unknown space, picks a safe spot there and sends it to the planner as the next goal.</li><li><strong>Flying:</strong> a tunnel controller follows the planned path by looking ahead along it, holds a safe altitude and keeps away from the walls.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/architecture.png" alt="System architecture from simulator and bridge through mapping, planning and flight control" /><figcaption>How the pieces connect: the simulator and bridge feed mapping, the planner and explorer decide where to go, and the controller turns the path into flight commands.</figcaption></figure><h2>Staying out of trouble</h2><p>Caves are full of dead ends, so the system is built to notice and recover rather than assume the plan always works.</p><ul><li>The planner keeps a safety margin around every obstacle and smooths the path so the drone never has to make a sharp turn.</li><li>The explorer rejects targets that sit too close to a wall.</li><li>If the drone is told to move but barely moves for a couple of seconds, it stops, reverses and turns to give the LiDAR a new view, then plans again.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/rviz-mission.jpg" alt="RViz showing the 3D map during a mission next to the planner and controller terminals" /><figcaption>A mission in progress: the 3D map in RViz, with the planner and flight-controller terminals alongside.</figcaption></figure><h2>Maps and test worlds</h2><p>The map is stored as small cubes, so the planner works with solid obstacles instead of millions of raw laser points. The system was exercised in several simulated worlds: a rough cave, a mine, a collapsed building and a warehouse.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/octomap-closeup.jpg" alt="Occupancy voxels around the drone in RViz" /><figcaption>Occupied space around the drone, close up.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/gazebo-warehouse.jpg" alt="The simulated quadrotor in a warehouse test world" /><figcaption>The simulated quadrotor in one of the test worlds.</figcaption></figure><h2>Main settings</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Map cell size</td><td>10 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Planner update</td><td>about every 0.8 s</td></tr><tr><td>Hover height</td><td>0.8 m</td></tr><tr><td>Top speed</td><td>0.8 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Look-ahead distance</td><td>1.0 m</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A single command starts the whole mission: the simulator, the transform bridge, mapping and planning, the flight controller and the explorer, each in its own terminal.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/cave-exploration-drone/rviz-displays.jpg" alt="RViz with the occupancy grid, path and TF displays enabled" /><figcaption>The RViz setup used while developing: occupancy grid, planned path and coordinate frames.</figcaption></figure><h2>Where it stands</h2><p>This is a simulation project. The repository documents the system and its tuning values, but there is no formal benchmark of success rate or area covered, so none is claimed here. The planning and control parts are separate from the simulator, so moving them to a real vehicle is a matter of replacing the simulated sensors and flight interface.</p><p>Code, launch instructions and configuration are in the <a href="https://github.com/MohammedBajhaw/drone-navigation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">project repository</a>.</p>$p$,
  updated_at = now()
where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone';

delete from public.project_media where project_id in (select id from public.projects where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone');
insert into public.project_media (project_id, storage_path, alt, sort_order) values
  ((select id from public.projects where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone'), $p$projects/cave-exploration-drone/tunnel-octomap.jpg$p$, $p$Coloured 3D voxel map of a tunnel built live from the drone's LiDAR$p$, 0),
  ((select id from public.projects where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone'), $p$projects/cave-exploration-drone/architecture.png$p$, $p$System architecture from simulator and bridge through mapping, planning and flight control$p$, 1),
  ((select id from public.projects where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone'), $p$projects/cave-exploration-drone/rviz-mission.jpg$p$, $p$RViz showing the 3D map during a mission next to the planner and controller terminals$p$, 2),
  ((select id from public.projects where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone'), $p$projects/cave-exploration-drone/octomap-closeup.jpg$p$, $p$Occupancy voxels around the drone in RViz$p$, 3),
  ((select id from public.projects where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone'), $p$projects/cave-exploration-drone/rviz-displays.jpg$p$, $p$RViz with the occupancy grid, path and TF displays enabled$p$, 4),
  ((select id from public.projects where slug = 'autonomous-cave-exploration-drone'), $p$projects/cave-exploration-drone/gazebo-warehouse.jpg$p$, $p$The simulated quadrotor in a warehouse test world$p$, 5);

update public.projects set
  title = $p$Robotic Arm: SolidWorks Design to Simscape Control$p$,
  subtitle = $p$A multi-link robotic arm designed in CAD, simulated with real physics in MATLAB, and driven to target points with inverse kinematics.$p$,
  summary = $p$A robotic arm taken from CAD design to physics simulation. SolidWorks models are imported into Simscape Multibody, inverse kinematics turn a target point into joint angles, and PID control is demonstrated on a simpler two-joint version.$p$,
  description = $p$Designing a robotic arm is more than drawing parts: you also need to know it can move smoothly, carry its own weight and reach the point you ask for. This project follows the whole path, from a multi-link arm designed in SolidWorks to a physics-based simulation in MATLAB Simscape Multibody.

Two models are included. A simple two-joint arm is used to learn PID tuning, and a larger five-part arm with a gripper is driven by inverse kinematics.$p$,
  status = $p$Completed$p$,
  start_date = $p$Jan 2026$p$,
  end_date = $p$Feb 2026$p$,
  tags = array[$p$Robotic Arm$p$,$p$Mechanical CAD$p$,$p$Physical Simulation$p$,$p$Inverse Kinematics$p$,$p$PID Control$p$]::text[],
  tools = array[$p$SolidWorks$p$,$p$MATLAB$p$,$p$Simulink$p$,$p$Simscape Multibody$p$,$p$Inverse Kinematics$p$,$p$PID Control$p$]::text[],
  outcomes = array[$p$Connected SolidWorks geometry and mass properties to a Simscape physics model$p$,$p$Wrote inverse kinematics that turn an XYZ target into joint angles$p$,$p$Demonstrated PID control on a two-joint learning model$p$,$p$Fixed the mass-import error that stops CAD models from simulating$p$]::text[],
  repository_url = $p$https://github.com/MohammedBajhaw/Robotic-Arm-SolidWorks-Matlab$p$,
  featured = true,
  rich_content = $p$<h2>What the project is about</h2><p>A robotic arm has to do more than look right: its parts must move smoothly, carry their own weight and reach the point you ask for. This project follows that whole path. The arm is designed in SolidWorks, brought into MATLAB as a physics model, and then given the maths to move to a target position.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/assembly-front.png" alt="The five-part robotic arm assembled from the SolidWorks models" /><figcaption>The arm assembled from the exported SolidWorks parts (rendered from the project's CAD files).</figcaption></figure><h2>From CAD to a physical model</h2><p>The arm was modelled as separate parts in SolidWorks and assembled. The assembly carries each part's mass, centre of mass and inertia, so when it is imported into Simscape Multibody the simulated arm feels gravity and joint loads like a real one. When the arm is stretched out, for example, the base joint really does need more torque to hold the pose.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/parts-sheet.png" alt="The individual SolidWorks parts of the arm" /><figcaption>The CAD parts: base, links, joint housing and the gripper finger, each shown on its own.</figcaption></figure><h2>Two models</h2><ul><li><strong>2-DOF tutorial arm:</strong> a simple two-joint arm used to study PID control, set a target point and watch how the gains change overshoot and settling.</li><li><strong>Advanced arm:</strong> a larger arm with a base turntable, shoulder, elbow, wrist and a two-finger gripper, moved by inverse kinematics.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/tutorial-2dof.png" alt="The simpler 2-DOF tutorial arm" /><figcaption>The 2-DOF tutorial arm.</figcaption></figure><h2>Telling the arm where to go</h2><p>Instead of guessing each joint angle, you give the arm a point in space (X, Y, Z in millimetres) and the inverse kinematics script works out the angles. The base turns toward the target, and the shoulder and elbow are solved from the arm's link lengths with simple trigonometry. The wrist is then tilted to keep the gripper level. If the target is out of reach, the script warns and clamps it to the arm's maximum reach.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/assembly-side.png" alt="Side view of the assembled robotic arm" /><figcaption>Side view of the arm.</figcaption></figure><h2>Making it simulate reliably</h2><ul><li>Setup scripts load the model, fix the base to the table, lock the gripper joints and add the angle inputs, so the model is ready with one command.</li><li>CAD imports often fail with a &ldquo;degenerate mass&rdquo; error when massless parts are joined directly. A tiny extra mass body between the joints (IntermediateMass) fixes it without changing the arm.</li></ul><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/robotic-arm/workflow.png" alt="Workflow from SolidWorks CAD through Simscape, inverse kinematics and PID control" /><figcaption>The workflow: CAD to physical model, target point to joint angles, and control on top.</figcaption></figure><h2>Where it stands</h2><p>The arm moves in step jumps to each target rather than along a smooth trajectory, and the joint-angle offsets between the maths and the CAD model are marked for calibration in the scripts. Natural next steps are smooth trajectory planning, camera-based object detection and moving the control to ROS 2 for hardware tests.</p><p>CAD files, MATLAB scripts and setup instructions are in the <a href="https://github.com/MohammedBajhaw/Robotic-Arm-SolidWorks-Matlab" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">project repository</a>.</p>$p$,
  updated_at = now()
where slug = 'robotic-arm-cad-simulation-control';

